 "grid_note": "HOW THE CELLS WERE LOCATED. The 39 vertical rules were detected on the PRINTED HEADING band of this sheet (y 430-610) as the 39 strongest thin-line maxima of a high-passed darkness profile, giving x = 684 ... 3691 at a pitch of 79.1 px. That template was CHECKED AGAINST THE PRINTED HEADING before a single cell was read: column 1 reads 'Under 5' under MALES, column 13 '100 and upwards', column 14 'Under 5' under FEMALES, column 26 '100 and upwards', and the male/female divide falls on rule 14 where the sheet carries a double rule. The rules of this book lean: the same template, correlated against each row band's own darkness profile, sits +2 px at line 1 and +38 px at line 30, so the column positions carry that lean rather than a single x. The 30 writing lines were fitted off the NAMES column ink as a single pitch (y = 659 + 75.6 n for the mark band, the name ink sitting about 25 px lower) and checked against the names themselves. Every column carrying a mark was then read a SECOND time as a montage of its own 30 cells, cut at the leaned rules, which is what separated the tally marks from three ink blots and a page-edge streak. No cell value here is an OCR output.",
 "cells_state": "partial",
 "cells_note": "Thirty-six of the thirty-eight columns balance against the figures the enumerator wrote at the foot of this sheet, including the twenty that are blank down the page and blank in the footing and are treated as balanced at zero. TWO DO NOT: free white females Under 5 (column 14) reads 14 against a printed 16, and free white females 5 under 10 (column 15) reads 18 against a printed 15. The two residuals are of opposite sign and nearly cancel (-2 and +3 against a footing pair of 31), which is the shape of marks read into the wrong one of two ADJACENT columns; the montage was cut at the leaned rules and re-read and did not move them, so the disagreement is left standing rather than forced. Columns 14 and 15 are therefore NOT committed: `cells` omits them and `cells_first_pass` carries the whole reading, balanced or not, so the next reader starts from a reading rather than from the sheet. It is NOT reconciled data and nothing downstream may treat it as such. THE ARITHMETIC ALSO CORRECTED TWO FOOTING READINGS: at a 1400 px contact view columns 5 and 6 read '25' and '16', and the line-by-line sums came to 29 and 19; zoomed on the footing itself both figures are plainly 29 and 19, so the sums stand and the low-resolution reading was wrong. The free coloured block carries exactly one mark on the whole sheet - line 10, column 36, free coloured females 36 under 55 - and the enumerator footed that column at 1.",
 "cells_columns_committed": 36,
